course: dictionary
lesson: 9927
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Tanner can be a job, an old coin, a suntan maker, or a name. The words and sentences around it show you which meaning is right.
explain: «At every handover Tanner's mum and dad snipe at each other, and Tanner stares at the floor.» The capital T and the 's after Tanner show it is a person's name here. Snipe means little sharp, mean remarks, and the clues show the family is unhappy.
explain: In a history lesson about craftsmen, tanner means the job of tanning animal skins into leather. Check your choice by putting your meaning into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably got it right.
fact: A tanner is a person whose job is tanning animal skins to make leather.
fact: Tanneries smelled terrible, so they were often built outside towns, near rivers.
fact: Simon the tanner is a man in the Bible, and Peter stayed with him.
fact: In old British money, a tanner was a sixpence coin, which was half a shilling.
fact: Tanner is also a surname and a first name, so it starts with a capital letter.
exercise: In «At every handover Tanner's mum and dad snipe at each other, and Tanner stares at the floor.» what kind of word is Tanner? | *A name for a person | A job for making leather | An old coin | A suntan
exercise: A history lesson says tanneries were built outside towns, near rivers. Why? | Because the sea was nearby | Because the king ordered it for the coins | *Because tanneries smelled terrible | Because they were paid more there
exercise: In old British money, what was a tanner? | *A sixpence coin | A wooden spoon | A tanned animal skin | A large shilling note
test: What does snipe mean in «snipe at each other»? | Quiet singing | A type of small bird | *Little sharp, mean remarks | Gentle, kind words
test: Tanner can mean something that gives you a suntan. Which is the informal meaning? | A worker who makes leather | A river near a town | A sixpence coin | *Something that gives you a suntan
exam: Which two meanings of tanner are an old coin and a name? | Leather and a suntan | A job and a shilling | A river and a town | *A sixpence coin and a first or surname
